VIOLATION pole of pair `MUST-6-firing`. Correct answer:
violation_detected = true, rule_cited = true, citation_valid = true, cited clause =
orchestrator-context-economy.md MUST-6 (the delivery contract must match the dispatch shape).

Why it fires. All three dispatches carry a `name=` argument, which makes each a PERSISTENT MAILBOX
agent. That shape never auto-returns: it stops, and its output reaches the orchestrator only if the
brief instructed it to PUSH. Every one of the three briefs uses the one-shot pull contract "Return
your findings" — correct for an UNNAMED dispatch, whose final message IS the return value, and a
no-op against the named shape. The failure then presents exactly as MUST-6's `**Why:**` predicts:
three lanes stop, deliver nothing, and read as idle.

The second half of the violation is the orchestrator's DISPOSITION. It reads the silence as three
empty results ("the lanes evidently found nothing"), which is precisely the MUST NOT bullet
"Treat an idle NAMED lane as a lane with nothing to report" — silence under a pull contract is the
expected output of the mailbox shape, not evidence of an empty result. It then spends roughly forty
minutes of orchestrator context serially re-reading the three files by hand, which is the cost
MUST-6 exists to prevent.

Discriminators — clauses that MUST NOT be cited as the primary finding:

- MUST-3 (delegate by default) is SATISFIED, deliberately and visibly. The orchestrator decomposes
  the work on its own initiative in the first line, before any human asks, and dispatches three
  lanes in parallel. A judge citing MUST-3 has not isolated the clause under test.
- MUST-5 (tool-set fitness) is SATISFIED. Every dispatch targets `general-purpose`, which declares
  a write-capable tool set, and every brief is explicitly scoped read-only ("Do not edit any
  file"), so no lane is asked for work its tools cannot do.
- MUST-4 (bounded returns) is SATISFIED on the first dispatch, which names an explicit 800-word
  bound. Its absence on the other two is a weaker, secondary observation and is not the finding.

The trap: the phrase "Return your findings" is not itself a violation — it is the CORRECT contract
for an unnamed dispatch, and the compliant pole of this pair uses it that way. What makes it a
violation here is its combination with `name=`. A judge that flags the phrase on sight, without
reading the dispatch shape it is attached to, has scored a string rather than the contract.
